clueweb09-en0000-47-08761.0 [] [][] [][][] [][][][] [][][] [][] [] [][] [][] [][][] [][][][] [][][] [][] [] [][] [][] [][][] [][][][] [][][] [][] [] [][] Lymphoma in dogs: Sometimes a dog owner will bring in a dog ... usually middle aged or older ... any breed, but often a Golden Retreiver ... Sometimes these dogs are sick and other times not, but your vet notices that multiple lymph nodes are enlarged. We're all trained to check for this. Your vet will strongly suspect the cancer known as Lymphoma ... something we see all too often in both cats and dogs. With blood work, urinalysis, radiographs, ultrasound, lymph node aspirates, and especially biopsy we can confirm the suspected diagnosis as well as determine to some degree the extent of the disease. Often multiple organs are affected. This is a serious disease; without treatment, patients often live only a few months after diagnosis. But good news; treatment is often sucessful. I'm sorry, but once we get a diagnosis I don't know a whole lot about the treatment of this disease. I refer these cases to our cancer specialist in the big city of Greenville. I do, however have a client information sheet copied below about
clueweb09-en0004-50-11405.0 [Animal Medical Center of New York] 510 East 62nd Street, New York, NY 10065 Phone: 212-838-8100 Home Contact Us For Appointments Make A Gift For Veterinarians SEARCH: Directions About Us Emergency Care Appointment Information Our Specialists and Medical Services Special Events Pet Profiles News Alerts Contact Us Employment Mitoxantrone and dacarbazine (DTIC) Study Name: Comparison of mitoxantrone and dacarbazine (DTIC) with mitoxantrone for treatment of resistant lymphoma in dogs Study population: Dogs Disease or Condition: Lymphoma Why is this study being done? We are enrolling dogs with lymphoma into a clinical study designed to evaluate a new chemotherapy protocol. Dogs with lymphoma that has become resistant to standard first-line therapy with CHOP (cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, and prednisone, with or without L-asparaginase)-based chemotherapy will be enrolled. Lymphoma is the most common tumor that we treat in dogs. The majority (>95%) eventually develop resistance to front-line chemotherapy drugs. For these dogs, there are no current standards-of-care. The chemotherapy drugs mitoxantrone and dacarbazine (also called DTIC) have both been shown to be effective in the rescue situation for dogs with lymphoma, and are well-tolerated. The combination of these two drugs has not been evaluated in dogs. There is potential for this combination to
