clueweb09-en0009-61-32953.0 [] CALIFORNIA Study: Evaluation of Genistein Combined Polysaccharide (GCP) Location: University of California Veterinary Hospital Davis, CA Phone: (530) 752-1393, ext 431 www.vmth.ucdavis.edu/vmth/services/oncology/oncology.html Eligibility: Dogs with high grade lymphoma that have not been treated with chemotherapy or steroids whose owners decline standard chemotherapy. Dogs must have enlarged peripheral lymph nodes, mild or no systemic clinical signs, and specific bloodwork values. Summary: High grade lymphoma represents approximately 80% of blood cancers in the dog and is the most commonly diagnosed and treated veterinary cancer. Despite treatment with aggressive multi-drug chemotherapy protocols, the median survival of dogs with high grade lymphoma is less than one year. Without treatment, life expectancy is only 4-6 weeks. GCP is a product of soy extract with no known toxicity which has shown anti-cancer activity in the laboratory. The purpose of this study is to determine the efficacy and safety of GCP for the treatment of dog high grade lymphoma. The side effects of GCP in the dog is unknown, but soft stool or diarrhea has occurred in other species at high doses. Requirements and incentives: Clients must be able to give oral medications (in capsule form) to their dogs. Follow-up at UC Davis is required at
