clueweb09-en0008-69-03213.0 HTTP/1.1 200 OK Accept-Ranges: bytes Content-Type: text/html Server: Apache Last-Modified: Sat, 17 Jan 2009 20:01:57 GMT ETag: "2e680df-26c0b-460b329752399" Date: Mon, 19 Jan 2009 06:18:13 GMT Connection: close Content-Length: 68241 DIET & SUPPLEMENTS FOR VARIOUS HEALTH PROBLEMS Arthritis (separate page) Kidney Disease (separate page) Cancer Liver Disease including information on low-purine diets for dogs with liver shunts Heart Disease Ear Problems Diarrhea IBD, IBS, Colitis, and other Gastrointestinal Disorders including information on EPI, SIBO and Addison's Disease Hypothyroidism Email Lists and Message Boards I want to preface this webpage by stating that I am not a vet, nor do I have any training in nutrition or other medical fields. The information I am presenting here has been gleaned over several years of study from books, from the internet, and from the knowledge and experience of others. It is not meant to replace your vet's advice or any medications they may prescribe, but hopefully will suggest additional options to think about, and other areas to explore, based on your dog's condition. I welcome any feedback, or any additional information you may want to share that can be added to this website. Arthritis This section has been moved to its own web page.
clueweb09-en0005-35-08642.0 [Canine Cancer,Cancer,K-9 Cancer,Dog Cancer,awareness,canine Lymphomacanine Osteosarcoma,canine Hemangiosarcoma,CafePress, non-profit, chemotherapy, pet care, dog health, diet, chemotherapy, ] [Canine Cancer Awareness. org] This site is dedicated to all those diagnosed and lost to cancer [Introduction] [Canine Cancers][Lymphoma][Osteosarcoma][Hemangiosarcoma][Adenocarcinoma][Mast Cell Tumor][Mammary Cancer][Transitional Cell ] [Chemo Drugs ][Holistic Therapy][Supplement Exchange!] [Laboratory Values ][Diet][Home Care Tips][Support Groups] [Faces Of Courage][Sponsors Needed] [You Can Help][Logo Items & Store][Thank You!] [NCCF][CCA Events][Past Announcements] [cca ribbon111.png] Welcome to the Canine Cancer Awareness (CCA) web site. Within these pages, we hope you find useful information to guide you on your journey with your dog in battling this disease. Our mission is to provide you with knowledge and support so that you can consult with your vet to get the best possible treatment for your dog. While you're here, please visit our gallery, Faces of Courage. There you will see those precious pets who have faced this disease. Some have wonderful tributes containing personal experiences. They, along with countless others, will never be forgotten, and are our driving force. CCA is a nonprofit organization dedicated to fighting canine cancer. We help sponsor the veterinary costs of dogs diagnosed with cancer for those families in financial need who would not otherwise be
clueweb09-en0009-78-32258.0 top Samoyed Health Links Page 1 DISCLAIMER The health links and material on this site are provided for educational and informational purposes only and are not intended to be a substitute for consultation with a veterinarian or other pet health care professional. The opinions presented on the links and in the material on this site reflect various sides of issues. No representation is made that any of the information provided on this web site or links from this web site is accurate. Additionally, any mention of commercial products or services within these web pages should not be viewed as an endorsement. Decisions with respect to the specific care and treatment of an animal should always be made with appropriate veterinary advice. HELP To keep these links up to date, I need your help. If you find a broken link, please e-mail me. There is no way I can check this number of links even on a weekly basis. Also, if you find a good link, please send it to me so that I can add it. I want these links to very up to date and usable for everyone. (PB-Mirage Samoyeds) REMINDER Don't forget to REFRESH your page every time
clueweb09-en0001-49-05848.0 Canine Stomach Cancer From LoveToKnow Dogs Canine stomach cancer accounts for only 1% of all cancers in dogs. The average age of diagnosis of this rare cancer is eight years old. Current studies indicate that male dogs are more likely to be diagnosed with stomach cancer than female dogs. [Black Airedale] [edit] Causes of Cancer in Dogs There are several different types of cancers that are associated with stomach cancer in dogs. [edit] Adenocarcinomas Adenocarcinomas are the type of tumors most often associated with cancer of the stomach. This cancer is one that is found in glandular tissue. This cancer will often spread to the liver, lungs, and lymph nodes. [edit] Mast Cell Tumors Mast cells are part of the immune system that have a role in inflammation and allergic responses. These cells are present in the linings of the digestive tract, lungs, nose, and skin. When mast cells become abnormal, they often form mast cell tumors. The tumors release excess amounts of the biological chemicals heparin and histamine which are normally produced by mast cells. This overdose of natural chemicals damages the body. [edit] Leiomyosarcomas Leiomyosarcomas are tumors that form in the walls of hollow organs such as the
clueweb09-en0011-34-11071.0 [petwalk.com logo link to home page Dog store, Reptile store, cat store, rabbit store, Pet directory] Monday, 19 January 2009 [cat hugs dog] [PetWalk.com menu] Home Pet Photos Pet Businesses Animal Shelter & Recues Product Reviews Coupons and Sales Free & Giveaways Newsletter Pet Education Contact Us Search Pet Care Tips Just for Fun Get Listed Forum Login Username Password Remember me Lost Password? No account yet? Register Secondhand Smoke Threatens Your Pet Saturday, 01 September 2007 [Sample Image]Secondhand smoke has been associated with oral cancer and lymphoma in cats, lung and nasal cancer in dogs, as well as lung cancer in birds. For years secondhand smoke has been known to threaten to nonsmokers health. Occording to the Center for Disease Control and Prevention, secondhand smoke is attributed with killing thousands of adult nonsmokers annually. If smoking is that harmful to human beings, it would make sense that secondhand smoke would have an adverse effect on pets that live in the homes of smokers, said Dr. Carolynn MacAllister, Oklahoma State University Cooperative Extension Service veterinarian. “There have been a number of scientific papers recently that have reported the significant health threat secondhand smoke poses to pets,” MacAllister said. “Secondhand smoke
clueweb09-en0011-48-08760.0 [PetWave] Login/Sign Up Tell a Friend Bookmark this Page Submit Feedback A-Z Guide Breeds Health Behavior Care & Nutrition Community Dog A-Z GuideCat A-Z Guide Dog Breed CenterCat Breed Center Dog Health CenterCat Health Center Dog Behavior CenterCat Behavior Center Dog Basic Care & NutritionCat Basic Care & Nutrition Home Dog A-Z Guide Dog Health Center Heart & Blood Lymphoma Diagnosis and Tests Diagnosis Lymphoma Overview and Facts Symptoms and Types Diagnosis and Tests Diagnosis Treatment and Care Top Ten Dog Health Guides Ear Infections Atopy Hot Spots Gastritis Diarrhea Urinary Tract Infection Skin Tumors Eye Inflammation Arthritis Hypothyroidism Lymphoma in Dogs Overview and Facts Symptoms and Types Diagnosis and Tests Treatment and Care Diagnosis Print Diagnosing Lymphoma Biopsy of affected lymph nodes or organs confirms the diagnosis, although a needle aspiration of an affected lymph node can increase suspicion of the disease. X-rays, ultrasound, blood analysis, and bone marrow biopsy reveal other locations of the cancer. The stage of the disease is important to treatment and prognosis. Stage I - only one lymph node or lymphoid tissue in one organ involved. Stage II - lymph nodes in only one area of the body involved. Stage III - generalized lymph
