clueweb09-en0005-30-16327.0 C O L D R I V E R V E T E R I N A R Y C E N T E R Integrated approach to lymphoma CASE REPORTS Allergies in a dog Allergies in a cat Arthritis Colitis. otitis Cushings disease Ear infections Feline chronic renal failure Heartworm Inflammatory bowel disease Kennel cough Kidney failure, disc disease Lymphangitis Lymphoma Oral papillomas Home page Many dogs and cats come to Cold River Veterinary Center with a history of chronic illness. They have multiple health problems and generally are not making progress with conventional treatment. Our job is to find the underlying problem and promote healing using non-toxic, natural therapies. Nikki is a good example of how the body can heal when given the right combination of nutrition and immune support. Eye problems[wpe1.jpg (102269 bytes)] Nikki is a 15 year old neutered male Toy poodle who was referred to us for treatment of central corneal defects in both eyes. Nikki had been vaccinated annually for distemper, parvovirus, corona virus, and kennel cough, with rabies boosters every two years. He was on a heartworm medication monthly. His caregiver provided him with a pet vitamin, glucosamine and chondroitin sulfate for degenerative
clueweb09-en0003-07-03503.0 Dog Cancer From Pet Health Learning Center Jump to: navigation, search Contents 1 Signs and symptoms 2 Diagnosis 3 Pathophysiology 4 Causes 5 Treatment 6 Suggested Products [edit] Signs and symptoms Cancer is a devastating disease and it's truly heartbreaking when it affects your beloved canine companion. As a pet owner, educating yourself about dog cancer can help you to catch the disease in its earlier stages and to provide your sick pet with the best care possible. Cancer occurs in dogs in a multitude of different forms, from liver cancer and kidney cancer to skin cancer and leukemia. With so many different types of canine cancer, it's impossible to produce a single list of symptoms to check for when on the lookout for cancer. Rather, each type of cancer has a separate collection of symptoms and warning signs that may arise when a dog is affected by a certain strain of the disease. While certain symptoms are common between several types of cancer, such as weight loss and loss of appetite, other signs are more unique to specific forms. One common type of this disease that occurs in canines is orophayngeal cancer, a type of mouth cancer. Weight loss
clueweb09-en0006-38-11136.0 [Basset Hound Veterinary Notes] Relative to other purebred dogs, basset hounds are a healthy breed. However, they have a genetic predisposition to a number of disorders that one should be aware of, both as breeders and as owners of bassets. The following articles and links are of particular medical interest to basset hound fanciers. Medical Disorders in the Basset Hound Glaucoma in the Basset Hound (by John S. Sleasman, DVM) Basset Hounds Shouldn't Bleed (by Carolyn Young) Hereditary Basset Hound Thrombopathy . . . 25 Years Later (by James L. Catalfamo Ph.D and Marjory B. Brooks D.V.M.) Veterinary Information on the Web Odie's Year with Cancer: a journal of one basset's struggle against canine lymphoma. BHCA's list of health-related links for basset hounds is particularly useful. A useful article on basset hound thrombopathia for the scientifically inclined Last updated November 30, 2008
