clueweb09-en0006-80-07264.0 Home | Site Map | Donate | Join ADA | Contact ADA | Shopping Cart [American Dietetic Association] Advanced Search [Food and Nutrition Information] [Careers and Students] [Conference and Events] [Advocacy and the Profession] [Continuing Education] [Media] [Shop Online] About ADA Journal/Publications Position Papers [Members Only - Please login or join] Foundation Dietetic Registration Accreditation/Dietetics Education Join Online/Member Benefits Corporate Relations Sponsorship ADA Groups Research International Practice Member Issues [Members Only - Please login or join] Online Discussions [Members Only - Please login or join] Evidence Analysis Library [Members Only - Please login or join] Nutrition Care Process [Members Only - Please login or join] My ADA Member ID Password Forgot your password? Which is Healthier, Butter or Margarine? Which is Healthier, Butter or Margarine? September 8, 2005 From a fat and calorie standpoint, butter and margarine are the same with about 35 calories and four grams of fat per teaspoon. Both are primarily fat; only the source differs. Butter contains more saturated fats than most margarine. Because margarine is made from vegetable oil, it has no cholesterol. For a spread with less saturated fat and minimal or no trans fat, buy soft tub or liquid margarine, rather than stick.
clueweb09-enwp03-14-13428.0 Margarine From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia (Redirected from Soft margarine) Jump to: navigation, search [] [] Margarine in a tub Margarine (pronounced /ýýmýýrdýýýýrýýn/, /ýýmýýrdýýýýriýýn/ or /ýýmýýrgýýriýýn/), as a generic term, can indicate any of a wide range of butter substitutes. In many parts of the world, margarine has become the best-selling table spread, although butter and olive oil also command large market shares. Margarine is an ingredient in the preparation of many other foods. In some regions people may refer to margarine as butter in informal speech, but in several countries laws forbid food packaging to refer to margarine as "butter". Recipes sometimes refer to margarine as oleo. Contents 1 History 1.1 Margarine in the United States 1.2 Margarine in Canada 2 Margarine today 3 Nutrition 3.1 Amount of fat 3.2 Saturated fat 3.3 Unsaturated fat 3.4 Trans fat 3.5 Cholesterol 3.6 Plant sterol/stanol esters 4 See also 5 References 6 External links [edit] History [] This article or section is written in an informal style and with a personally invested tone. It reads more like a story than an encyclopedia entry. To meet Wikipedia's quality standards and conform with our Neutral Point of View policy, this article or section
